  "body": "There seems to be an inconsistency in how Chrome 101.0.4951.72 (Chrome OS) and Firefox 101.0 (Linux) handle `<form method=\"dialog\">` when a shadow boundary separates it and the `<dialog>` element.\r\n\r\nConsider the following page:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html>\r\n  <head>\r\n    <script>\r\n      window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {\r\n        const dialog = document.querySelector('dialog');\r\n        dialog.innerHTML = '<div></div>';\r\n        const shadow = dialog.firstChild.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });\r\n        shadow.innerHTML = '<form method=\"dialog\"><button>Close</button></form>';\r\n      });\r\n    </script>\r\n  </head>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <dialog open></dialog>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn Chrome, clicking the button closes the dialog: the dialog's default behavior is to submit its ancestor form, and the form closes the dialog.\r\n\r\nIn Firefox, clicking the button doesn't appear to do anything. If I instead make the form be a direct child of the dialog, the button closes the dialog, so I'm guessing that the shadow root is the reason for the difference:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<!DOCTYPE html>\r\n<html>\r\n  <body>\r\n    <dialog open>\r\n      <form method=\"dialog\">\r\n        <button>Close</button>\r\n      </form>\r\n    </dialog>\r\n  </body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt's not clear to me from https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#submit-dialog what the expected behavior is. In \"Let _subject_ be the nearest ancestor `dialog` element of _form_,\" does \"nearest ancestor\" permit crossing shadow boundaries?",
